[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc2
diagnosis_text:
The network path between the two desks is healthy; the failure is on PC2 itself at the application layer. Evidence:
- L2/L3 connectivity is fine: pc1↔pc2 ping succeeds with 0% loss and sub‑3 ms RTT in both directions, and both hosts have eth0 up in 10.1.0.0/24 with correct defaults.
- From pc1, HTTP probes to 10.1.0.101 return immediately without data. On pc2, netstat -tuln shows no services listening on 0.0.0.0 (only the local 127.0.0.11 resolver is present), so there is no file‑sharing daemon bound to any address.
This explains the symptom: ICMP pings work (so “the wire/switch” is fine), but TCP connection attempts to whatever service pc1 used “yesterday” are refused or time out because nothing is listening on pc2. The access switches (aswa2/aswa3) and uplinks to the distribution layer are not the cause.

Affected component: Application/service layer on PC2 (the file-sharing server process). 
Mechanism: With no listener on PC2 (e.g., SMB/445 or HTTP/80), connection attempts from PC1 get reset/timeout while ICMP still succeeds. 
Localization: Host pc2; no listening sockets on required ports. 
Root cause: The file-sharing service on PC2 was stopped/never started after a change or reboot. 
Corrective fix: Restart or re-enable the intended sharing service on pc2 (e.g., start smbd if using SMB; or httpd/python http.server if using HTTP), and set it to start at boot. Verify with netstat that the service is LISTENing and retest from pc1. No switch reconfiguration is required. 
=== END ===